Announcing the release of JX 1.0.0

JX is a full-featured C++ application framework and GUI class library
for X Windows.  The power of JX stems from its rich feature set and
extensibility which support all aspects of professional quality
application development.

The features built into JX include a generic messaging system to
support the Model-View-Controller paradigm, document management and
interaction, the ability to connect to multiple X displays, a powerful
word processor class, very extensive table support, background tasks,
and seamlessly integrated postscript printing.

This rich feature set does not impede the extensibility of the system,
however.  The base class for all GUI objects, the Widget, provides
mechanisms for supporting all of the actions and interactions that are
expected in today's commercial applications.  In addition, it is easy
to derive customized classes from almost any class in the library,
from the base Widget class to specialized classes such as Tables and
Menus.

JX is designed to make it easy to construct commercial quality
applications.  All the common, required functionality is built in, so
developers need only connect the standard components together and then
develop custom Widgets that are unique to their particular
applications.

More information about JX is available at:

  http://www.cco.caltech.edu/~jafl/jx/

To subscribe to the mailing list, send email to:

  majordomo@cco.caltech.edu

and include "subscribe jx" in the body of the message.

The tar file for JX 1.0.0 has been uploaded to sunsite and should soon
be available in:

  ftp://sunsite.unc.edu/pub/Linux/X11/devel/JX-1.0.0_gcc-2.7.2.tar.gz

The rpm file is available in:

  ftp://ftp.redhat.com/pub/contrib/RPMS/JX-1.0.0-1.i386.rpm
  ftp://ftp.redhat.com/pub/contrib/SRPMS/JX-1.0.0-1.src.rpm

License:

JX is free for non-commercial use.  Details of the license are
available at:

  http://www.cco.caltech.edu/~jafl/sypp/

Related software:

The first publicly available program written with JX is a free C/C++
code development environment called Code Crusader (jcc) which we are
developing into a clone of the MetroWerks CodeWarrior system.
Currently, it features a text editor and a C++ class browser that
cooperate to streamline the process of developing code.  It is
included in the tar distribution of JX and is also available as a
separate rpm.

