Konnichi wa!

The rot13 text file ciphering utility is now available to the public.

It has been uploaded to /incoming/Linux at sunsite and should be moving
to /pub/Linux/utils/text soon.  It is also available from my (unfinished)
website: http://www.gnat.net/~anvil/ocode.html.

This little utility takes an input file and encodes it using the ROT13
cipher.  I know this is of marginal (if any) actual value, but I have
wanted to make some contribution to the Linux community since discovering
Linux 2 years ago...and it does have some nifty features.

	* Display total characters in file, characters encoded, and
		percentage of characters encoded.
	* Optionally, display runtime in clock-ticks.
	* Quiet mode (turn off screen output).
	* Ability to delete source file after ciphering.
	* On-screen help AND man page
	* Optional modified ROT13 cipher that uses a checksum generated
		from the source file. This defeats conventional ROT13.

The archive contains a Linux binary, a man page, source code, a test
file, installation instructions, and a copy of the GNU Public License.
The source code is straight ANSI C and should compile on any C/C++ compiler,
including DOS compilers.

Here's the .lsm entry. . .

Begin3
Title:          rot13 File Cypher Utility
Version:        1.2
Entered-date:   March 08, 1997
Description:    This is a small, simple, command-line utility
		for encoding and decoding files using the ROT13 cipher.
		It has options for specifying the output filename,
		deleting the input file after processing, suppressing
		screen output.  You can also specify that a modified
		ROT13 routine be used to defeat decoding by regular ROT13
		routines such as those in Netscape and newsreaders.
Keywords:       rot13, unix, linux, encode, decode, encrypt, decrypt,
		cipher, decipher, text
